<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhang Juzheng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1525">1525</a>–<a href="/years/1582">1582</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhang Juzheng was a Chinese scholar-official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> who, as senior grand secretary from 1572 until his death, was the de facto ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the early reign of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguang">Huguang</a>, he passed the palace examination in 1547 and became a grand secretary in 1567. With the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> Feng Bao and the Empress Dowager Li he streamlined the administration, enforced the single whip reform of taxes paid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, began the first nationwide land survey since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and built up a surplus. After his death most of his reforms were reversed and in 1584 his family's property was confiscated; he was rehabilitated in 1622.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-juzheng.jpg" alt="Zhang Juzheng" width="330" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rongshi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:張居正-清晰版.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Wanli Emperor (r. 1572–1620) began with able advisers, among them the Grand Secretary <b>Zhang Juzheng</b>, but grew tired of his quarrelling ministers and stayed behind the walls of the Forbidden City, where officials had to bribe powerful eunuchs to have their messages carried to him.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
